h d2 and d3, outcome=\"fail\" (since no unresolved, condition satisfied). Else outcome=\"pass\".\nWe run ddmin. Starting set size 8. The algorithm (from Zeller's delta debugging) splits set into complement sets and tests. Typically it tries to remove first half, if that still fails, continue with that half, else try other half, etc. The exact test count depends on implementation. But we can implement a standard ddmin (the one from the paper \"Yesterday, my program worked...\"). I recall the ddmin algorithm:\n\n```\ndef ddmin(c):\n    return _ddmin(c, set())\ndef _ddmin(c, r):\n    if len(c) == 1:\n        return c\n    n = len(c) // 2\n    c1 = c[:n]\n    c2 = c[n:]\n    if test(c1 | r) == FAIL:\n        return _ddmin(c1, r)\n    elif test(c2 | r) == FAIL:\n        return _ddmin(c2, r)\n    else:\n        # try to remove c1 from c\n        c1' = c - c1 (i.e., c2) with r? Actually standard: if test(c2 | r) == FAIL then ... else try to reduce granularity.\n        # Actually the algorithm is recursive with granularity. Simpler: use the iterative version.\n```\n\nGiven the complexity, maybe the intended solution is simpler. The task is \"SE-DD-01\" - possibly a first exercise in delta debugging where you just compute the union as described. But the output schema includes \"tests\" which is required. In the fixture, we don't see expected values. Perhaps we can look at the case_id: \"fg1-se-dd-01-b-000\", the 'b' might refer to a variant where the test function is deterministic. The payload includes \"_case_nonce\" which might be used to seed something, but we cannot use randomness.\n\nI'm leaning toward the union approach because it's straightforward and fits the minimal failure concept. But the \"tests\" field makes me hesitate. Could the \"tests\" be the number of items that are not in the minimal set? Or the total number of items? For case 0: 8 items, minimal 2, tests could be 6? Not sure.\n\nLet's examine the term \"unresolved_without\" - it suggests items that if m